  task and you find out. With one user and no server, the only person who can change a task is the person who'd receive
  the notification — and telling you about the edit you just made is noise, not information.

  So the design hinges on where change events actually come from that the user doesn't already know about.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what counts as a notification-worthy change.

  - [1mTime-crossing events[0m (task becomes due, goes overdue, a reminder fires) — the only genuinely new information in a
    single-user app: the [3mclock[0m changed, not the user. Needs a due-date field on tasks, a scheduler, and a decision about
    notifications while the tab is closed. This is the one that justifies the subsystem existing.
  - [1mUser's own edits[0m (completed, moved, deleted) — cheap, no clock, no permissions; but it's an activity log or an undo
    toast, not a notification. Low value, and calling it "notifications" sets up a misleading structure.
  - [1mBoth, over a shared event bus[0m — one event model, time events and edit events as two producers. Slightly more
    structure now; means a future sync/backend becomes a third producer rather than a rewrite. This is the design that
    makes "subsystem" the right word.

  [1mMy recommendation: time-crossing events, built on a small event bus[0m — i.e. the third option, scoped so edit events
  exist as a producer but the UI only surfaces time events initially. Time events are the only ones carrying
  information; the bus is maybe thirty lines and is what stops this becoming a pile of [38;5;153msetTimeout[39ms. If you tell me it's
  really just the second option, that's fine — but then it's an activity feed and I'd rather name it that.
